  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Decent Work principles are integrated in Sudan&apos;s national development framework through better capacities of stakeholders in the areas of labour market and employment policies</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The conflict in Sudan that broke out in April 2023 has caused widespread challenges that aggravated the humanitarian situation in the country, causing a shortage of food, water, cash, fuel, healthcare, and other basic services and triggering massive displacement within and out of the country. 
As a result of the challenging situation, the ILO and PROSPECTS partners reprogrammed their activities and interventions to new locations (White Nile and Kassala states) that have been receiving large scale displacement in addition to the existing refugee&#x2019;s population. These two states host a mixed situation of protracted displaced people, secondarily displaced refugees, newly displaced IDPs and vulnerable host communities. While responding to immediate needs, the PROSPECTS&#x2019; partners activities will be designed with a developmental lens by sustaining local life-saving services (education, health, and social welfare) and strengthening the capacity of local services. Local Economic Development Committees were established by the ILO in East Darfur and West Kordofan states to support in planning and monitoring of activities/interventions. 
The CPO intends to consolidate the work carried out under phase I of the PROSPECTS project and under phase II ILO will take the lead in establishing the Local Economic Development Committees (LEDCs) in the new targeted states of White Nile and Kassala to collaborate with and support the partners in the identification of activities, planning, implementation and monitoring.</narrative>
  </description>
